<h2 id="proposer">What already exists, and what this page is about</h2>

<p>The Reality Code Engine already holds the half of the problem that decides. Its public paper, <a href="https://research.openie.dev/papers/rce/">The Reality Code Engine</a>, describes the design of that half: a commit gate that checks every candidate at entry, exact replay (the runtime state is a left fold of the transition function over the log), projection of one Form to other languages, and energy accounting in <code>est_j</code>, an estimate in joules from a published cost table, kept apart from counter readings (<code>reported_j</code>) and wall-meter readings (<code>measured_j</code>). Some theorems about it are checked in Lean 4, a proof assistant, and the specification and Lean library are public. The measured runs reported in that paper are not all reproducible from the public repositories yet, so this page quotes no number from them and treats them as design context, not as evidence for any hypothesis below.</p>

<p><strong>This page is about the proposer:</strong> the learned part that suggests which Form to try. The gate, replay, projections and <code>est_j</code> are built and measured; the proposer is not.</p>

<h3 id="steer">The model steers; the engine renders</h3>

<p>Robotics already works this way. In V-JEPA 2-AC, the action-conditioned version of the V-JEPA 2 video model, a planner searches over robot actions by minimizing the distance between a predicted future representation and the representation of a goal image; it never generates pixels [B1]. The encoder was pretrained on more than one million hours of video, and the action-conditioned predictor on fewer than 62 hours of unlabeled robot video [B1, abstract]. RCE-JEPA proposes the same division for code: a learned model chooses edits to a Form, and the engine renders every target language from the accepted Form. No model emits code.</p>

<p>Code differs from robotics in one decisive way. A robot's world model must <em>learn</em> how the world moves, <span set:html={m(R`z_{t+1}=g_\phi(z_t,a_t)`)} />. In RCE the transition function <span set:html={m(R`\delta`)} /> is given and proved, so the forward dynamics need not be learned at all. What remains to learn is the inverse: which edit moves a candidate toward what the intent requires. MuZero learns a model because, as its authors put it, planning methods "rely on knowledge of the environment's dynamics" [T1, Sec. 1]; here those dynamics are written down.</p>

<h3 id="one-form">One real Form</h3>

<p>Before the architecture, the object. This is a complete program from the engine repository (<code>crates/rce/examples/surface/counter.rce</code>), fourteen lines in RCE's surface syntax, shown unedited:</p>

<pre class="mt-4 p-4 rounded bg-black/40 text-xs leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto"><code>{`intent "a counter that never goes negative"
    example inc, inc, dec -> 1
    example dec -> refused: Underflow

state Counter
    n: u64 = 0

event Inc
event Dec

error Underflow

step Counter on Inc -> n + 1
step Counter on Dec -> try n.checked_sub(1) else Underflow`}</code></pre>

<p>Read it as <span set:html={m(R`P=(S,E,\delta,D,\lambda)`)} />. The <code>state</code> block is S, one unsigned 64-bit integer starting at zero. The two <code>event</code> lines are E. The two <code>step</code> lines are <span set:html={m(R`\delta`)} />. The <code>intent</code> block is a small specification of intent, written <span set:html={m(R`\sigma(\iota)`)} /> on this page: two required traces, one of which must be refused. A decrement at zero is refused with <code>Underflow</code> and, by T8, leaves the state unchanged. The proposer's task, stated on this example: given only the <code>intent</code> block, find the <code>state</code>, <code>event</code> and <code>step</code> lines that the gate accepts.</p>

