<h3 id="specification-cost">The specification cost, named</h3>
<p>The checker guarantees that a Form is valid; it does not guarantee that it is the Form that was meant. In verified LLM-driven synthesis for concept design, invariant-only synthesis "reached verified designs quickly but often produced inconsistent designs across runs", which shows "that invariants alone underconstrain the design task"; scenario-guided synthesis recovered intended designs more consistently than natural-language prompting, although minimal scenarios could overfit [B27, abstract], on 3 applications and 12 variants with one LLM configuration [B27, Sec. 5]. The dominant failures "were not failures of bounded verification, but failures of specification coverage" [B27, Sec. 5.6]. In seL4 the abstract specification took about 4 person-months and the proof about 20 person-years (Klein et al. 2009, Section 2 below). A closed program world makes acceptance exact and search tractable. It moves the uncertainty of intent into <span set:html={m(R`\sigma(\iota)`)} />; it does not remove it. Falsifier RJ-F2 below makes that cost public and measurable.</p>

<h3 id="h11-h13">Proposer hypotheses</h3>
<p>The proposer is tested by H11 (monotone energy), H12 (decision-head calibration) and H13 (discrete against continuous latents). All thirteen hypotheses, H1 to H13, are stated in numerical order with their thresholds in one table in Section 5 below, under the same conventions: preregistered before the run, frozen engine commit and binary hash recorded, same hardware for every arm.</p>
<p>The H3 control is fixed now: the same gate and the same <code>est_j</code> accounting, with Qwen2.5-Coder-1.5B proposing surface code, the arm already run in the RCE paper (Sec. 5.5), where the gate admitted 3 of 16 proposed equivalences.</p>

<p id="falsifiers-moved"><strong>Falsifiers.</strong> The falsifiers for the premise itself, RJ-F1 to RJ-F5, now follow the hypotheses they test, directly after the H1 to H13 table in <a href="#falsifiers">Section 5 below</a>.</p>

<h3 id="risks">Risks and mitigations</h3>
<div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto"><table class={tbl}>
  <thead><tr><th>Risk</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Mitigation</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Decoding a latent amplifies small errors</td><td>Direct generation in a frozen JEPA latent fails dramatically for motion, from spectral ill-conditioning and decoder amplification [B20]</td><td>Never decode a latent; the proposer emits typed edits and the gate checks them</td></tr>
    <tr><td>A representation that does not collapse but learns the wrong thing</td><td>"Physical representation laziness" under SIGReg [B15]</td><td>Auxiliary exact-property heads; an inverse-dynamics regularizer</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Isotropic prior misfits a low-dimensional Form space</td><td>[B13]; [B14]</td><td>SIGReg and contrastive inverse-dynamics arms side by side, remembering that the inverse-dynamics arm was weaker on discrete puzzle state [B14]</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Energy terms not commensurate</td><td>[T2, Sec. 1.3]</td><td>Fit <span set:html={m(R`\alpha,\beta,\gamma`)} /> per experiment and publish them</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Decision head degrades on padded state</td><td>Documented weakness of the class [B8, B12]</td><td>Feed only canonical Form JSON and trace</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Expressiveness ceiling</td><td>Rice's theorem [T3]; the restricted language</td><td>Lift coverage is the measurement; RJ-F1 is the falsifier</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Specification cost dominates</td><td>seL4 effort (Section 2 below); invariant under-constraint [B27]</td><td>RJ-F2; tools that capture traces from running programs and infer contracts, as deliverables in their own right</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>

<p>The phased pilot plan, from the data engine through convergence, with preregistration and exit criteria for each phase, is kept with the project's working notes and summarized here: Phase 0 builds the lift corpus and receipt dataset; Phase 1 trains the Form encoder and tests H1, H5 and H11; Phase 2 builds and calibrates the decision head (H12); Phase 3 trains the proposer and planner against the token control (H3, H4, H10, H13); Phase 4 measures energy and determinism on a wall meter (H2, H8, H9); Phase 5 runs the convergence protocol, in which promotion takes the model out of the loop for each closed shape. Alongside Phase 0, a slice of the vericoding benchmark (source C34, listed under Convergence by commitment below), chosen as its state-machine-shaped tasks in Dafny, Verus and Lean, is lifted into Forms and scored under RCE's gate next to the published vericoding results, an outside benchmark for the step from specification to Form (H6, H7 and H8).</p>

